      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Clinical trials to date have not provided definitive evidence regarding the effects of glucose lowering with coronary heart disease (CHD) risk among diabetic patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "RESEARCH DESIGN AND METHODS",
          "content": "We prospectively investigated the association of HbA1c at baseline and during follow-up with CHD risk among 17,510 African American and 12,592 white patients with type 2 diabetes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "During a mean follow-up of 6.0 years, 7,258 incident CHD cases were identified. The multivariable-adjusted hazard ratios of CHD associated with different levels of HbA1c at baseline (<6.0 [reference group], 6.0-6.9, 7.0-7.9, 8.0-8.9, 9.0-9.9, 10.0-10.9, and ≥11.0%) were 1.00, 1.07 (95% CI 0.97-1.18), 1.16 (1.04-1.31), 1.15 (1.01-1.32), 1.26 (1.09-1.45), 1.27 (1.09-1.48), and 1.24 (1.10-1.40) (P trend = 0.002) for African Americans and 1.00, 1.04 (0.94-1.14), 1.15 (1.03-1.28), 1.29 (1.13-1.46), 1.41 (1.22-1.62), 1.34 (1.14-1.57), and 1.44 (1.26-1.65) (P trend <0.001) for white patients, respectively. The graded association of HbA1c during follow-up with CHD risk was observed among both African American and white diabetic patients (all P trend <0.001). Each one percentage increase of HbA1c was associated with a greater increase in CHD risk in white versus African American diabetic patients. When stratified by sex, age, smoking status, use of glucose-lowering agents, and income, this graded association of HbA1c with CHD was still present."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The current study in a low-income population suggests a graded positive association between HbA1c at baseline and during follow-up with the risk of CHD among both African American and white diabetic patients with low socioeconomic status."
        }
      ]},
